     
61 Problem :-
Given the head of a linked list, rotate the list to the right by k places.

Example 1:
Input: head = [1,2,3,4,5], k = 2
Output: [4,5,1,2,3]

Example 2:
Input: head = [0,1,2], k = 4
Output: [2,0,1]
 
Constraints:
The number of nodes in the list is in the range [0, 500].
-100 <= Node.val <= 100
0 <= k <= 2 * 109

============================================================================================================================≠=========

Solution:-
Intuition
To rotate the linked list to the right by k places, we need to move the last k nodes to the front.
Instead of shifting nodes one by one (which would be inefficient), we can:
Find the length of the list
Convert it into a circular linked list
Break the list at the correct position to form the rotated list

Approach
Traverse the list to find its length (len) and the last node (tail).
Connect the tail to the head → making the list circular.
Compute effective rotations using:
k = k % len
Find the new tail:
The node after the new tail becomes the new head.
Break the circular link by setting:
newTail->next = NULL

Complexity
Time complexity:O(n)
Space complexity:O(1)

Code :-
class Solution{
    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head,int k){
        if(head==null||head.next==null||k==0) return head; // edge

        int len=1;
        ListNode tail=head;
        while(tail.next!=null){ tail=tail.next; len++; } 

        k%=len;
        if(k==0) return head; 

        tail.next=head; // circular

        int steps=len-k;
        ListNode newtail=head;
        for(int i=1;i<steps;i++) newtail=newtail.next; 

        ListNode newhead=newtail.next; // head
        newtail.next=null;

        return newhead;
    }
}
